```markdown
Proof. The proof is analogous to the proof of (Kipnis and Landim, 1999, Chapter 11 Prop 2.3). First recall

\begin{align*} M_{t}^{N, f} &= Y_t(f) - Y_0(f) - \int_0^t Y_s(\mathcal{L}^N f) ds \\
N_{t}^{N, f} &= (M_{t}^{N, f})^2 - \int_0^t \Gamma^N f(s) ds. \end{align*}

With a slight abuse of notation, we interpret $M_{t}^{N, f}$ similarly $M_{t}^{N, f}, N_{t}^{N, f}, N_{t}^{N, f}$ here as a function $\mathscr{D}([0, T], (C^\infty)) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$. When writing $\mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{N, f}$ we mean the expectation of this function as a function of $Y^N$ and when writing $EM_{t}^{N, f}$ we regard it as a function of the random element of $\mathscr{D}([0, T], (C^\infty))$ with law $\mathcal{L}^*$. Fix $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$. We want to show that under $\mathcal{L}^*$

\begin{align*} \mathbb{E}[M_{t}^{N, f} | \mathscr{F}_s] &= M_{s}^{N, f} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbb{E}[N_{t}^{N, f} | \mathscr{F}_s] = N_{s}^{N, f}. \end{align*}

Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}, s \geq 0, 0 \leq s_1 \leq \cdots \leq s_n \leq s, H_1, \ldots, H_n \in C^\infty, \Psi \in C_b(\mathbb{R}^*)$ and define

\begin{align*} I: \quad &\mathscr{D}([0, T], (C^\infty))' \rightarrow \mathbb{R} \\
&Y \mapsto \Psi(Y_{s_1}(H_1), \ldots, Y_{s_n}(H_n)). \end{align*}

Now it suffices to show that

\begin{align*} \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{N, f} I(Y) = EM_{t}^{f} I(Y), \quad \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{E}_N N_{t}^{N, f} I(Y) = EN_{t}^{f} I(Y), \end{align*}

since then by the martingale property of $M_{t}^{N, f}$

\begin{align*} EM_{t}^{f} I(Y) = \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{N, f} I(Y) = \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{N, f} I(Y) = EM_{t}^{f} I(Y) \end{align*}

and analogous for the $N_{t}^{N, f}$ case, which implies that $M_{t}^{f}$ and $N_{t}^{f}$ are martingales under $\mathcal{L}^*$.

We start with the first martingale. First we show that we can replace $M_{t}^{N, f}$ by $M_{t}^{f}$ in the first expectation in (23). Using Jensen we see that

\begin{align*} (\mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{N, f} I(Y) - \mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{f} I(Y))^2 \leq \| \Psi \|^2_{\infty} \mathbb{E}_N (M_{t}^{N, f} - M_{t}^{f})^2 = \| \Psi \|^2_{\infty} \mathbb{E}_N \left(\int_0^t Y_s(\Delta_m f) ds - \int_0^t Y_s(\mathcal{L}^N f) ds \right)^2, \end{align*}

which goes to $0$ by lemma 3.1. Now it remains to show that

\begin{align*} \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{E}_N M_{t}^{f} I(Y) = EM_{t}^{f} I(Y). \end{align*}

First of all note that

\begin{align*} \mathbb{E}_N (M_{t}^{f})^2 &\leq 4 \left(\mathbb{E}_N Y_t(f^2) + \mathbb{E}_N Y_0(f^2) + \mathbb{E}_N \left(\int_0^t Y_s(\Delta_m f) ds \right)^2 \right) \\
&\leq 2\rho(1-\rho) \int f^2 d\nu + \rho^2(1-\rho) \int (\Delta_m f)^2 d\nu + o(1), \end{align*}

which implies that there exists $C > 0$ such that

\begin{align*} \sup_N \mathbb{E}_N (M_{t}^{f})^2 \leq \| \Psi \|^2_{\infty} \sup_N \mathbb{E}_N (M_{t}^{f})^2 \leq C < \infty. \end{align*}

This implies that the $M_{t}^{f}(Y)$ under $\mathcal{L}^N$ are uniformly integrable, so it suffices to show that $M_{t}^{f}(Y)$ under $\mathcal{L}^N$ converges to $M_{t}^{f}(Y)$ under $\mathcal{L}^*$ in distribution. We proceed in steps. First of all, consider the mapping

\begin{align*} P_1: D([0, T], (C^\infty)) &\rightarrow D([0, T], \mathbb{R})^{n+2} \\
Y &\mapsto (Y_-(Y), Y(\Delta_m f), Y(H_1), \ldots, Y(H_n)) \end{align*}

```

The LaTeX tag for chemical formulas in images is typically represented using the `chemfig` package. This package allows for the creation of chemical structures in a visually appealing manner. The tag for a chemical formula in LaTeX would look something like this:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chemfig}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
  \setchemfig{atom sep=2em}
  \chemfig{N(-[2]O)(-[6]O)-[:-30]N(-[2]H)(-[6]H)-[:-30]N(-[2]H)(-[6]H)}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This code will produce a chemical structure similar to the one shown in the image. The `chemfig` package provides a simple and intuitive way to draw chemical structures, making it a popular choice for chemists and educators.

The LaTeX tag for chemical formulas in images is typically represented using the `chemfig` package. For the chemical structure shown, the LaTeX code would be:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chemfig}

\begin{document}

\setchemfig{atom sep=2em}
\chemfig{R-O-[:-30]C(-[:-30]H)(-[:-90]H)(-[:-30]H)(-[:-30]H)(-[:-30]H)(-[:-30]H)-[:-30]OH}

\end{document}
```

This code will render the chemical structure of the compound in the image.

The LaTeX tag for chemical formulas in images is typically represented using the `chemfig` package. This package allows for the creation of chemical structures in a visually appealing manner. The tag for chemical formulas in images is usually written as `\chemfig{...}`. For example, the chemical structure shown in the image can be represented using the following LaTeX code:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chemfig}

\begin{document}

\chemfig{HO-S(=O)(=O)O}

\end{document}
```

This code will render the chemical structure of the compound shown in the image.
